  "result": "The command now prints **27.06** (it printed 26.09 before), and all 6 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** in `shop/money.py`, `to_cents` padded the decimal part on the wrong side. It used `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")`, which turns `\"5\"` into `\"05\"`, so `12.5` was read as 1205 cents instead of 1250. Any price with only one decimal place was affected this way.\n\n**Fix:** I changed it to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`. The math now checks out: 1250 \u00d7 2 = 2500, plus 8.25% tax (206) = 2706, which prints as 27.06.\n\nI also checked other inputs: `12` \u2192 1200, `12.05` \u2192 1205, `12.34` \u2192 1234, `0.1` \u2192 10 and `12.` \u2192 1200.\n\npytest isn't installed here, so I ran the tests with `python3 -m unittest`. None of the tests cover `to_cents`, which is why they didn't catch this bug. I didn't add a test for it.",
